::PC_U03_L05_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L05_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.11
Question:
What is sin(150 degrees)?
{
=A. 1/2#Correct. 150 degrees has reference angle 30 degrees and sine is positive in Quadrant II.
~B. -1/2#This uses the wrong sign for Quadrant II sine.
~C. sqrt(3)/2#This is the cosine magnitude for a 30-degree reference angle.
~D. -sqrt(3)/2#This uses the cosine magnitude and wrong sign.
}
::PC_U03_L05_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L05_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.11
Question:
What is tan(90 degrees)?
{
~A. 0#This would require sine to be 0, but sin(90)=1.
=B. undefined#Correct. tan(theta)=sin(theta)/cos(theta), and cos(90)=0.
~C. 1#Tangent is not 1 when cosine is 0.
~D. -1#This is not possible because tangent is undefined at 90 degrees.
}
::PC_U03_L05_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L05_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.11
Question:
What is sec(pi)?
{
~A. 0#Secant is reciprocal cosine, not 0 here.
~B. 1#cos(pi)=-1, so sec(pi) is not 1.
=C. -1#Correct. sec(pi)=1/cos(pi)=1/(-1)=-1.
~D. undefined#Secant is defined because cos(pi) is not 0.
}
::PC_U03_L05_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L05_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.11
Question:
Which sign should cos(240 degrees) have?
{
~A. positive#240 degrees is in Quadrant III, where cosine is negative.
~B. zero#Cosine is not 0 at 240 degrees.
~C. undefined#Cosine is defined for 240 degrees.
=D. negative#Correct. Cosine is negative in Quadrant III.
}
::PC_U03_L05_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L05_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.11
Question:
What is csc(pi/2)?
{
=A. 1#Correct. csc(pi/2)=1/sin(pi/2)=1/1=1.
~B. 0#This confuses cosecant with cosine.
~C. -1#The sine value at pi/2 is positive, so cosecant is not negative.
~D. undefined#Cosecant is defined because sine is not 0.
}